  "source_document": {
    "document_id": "99e116f8-c639-4d21-bb15-d36e08717040",
    "origin_url": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/27/used-cybertruck-prices-jump-after-tesla-kills-it/",
    "collected_at": "2026-11-27T19:18:51.094132+00:00",
    "origin_id": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/27/used-cybertruck-prices-jump-after-tesla-kills-it/",
    "canonical_url": "https://electrek.co/2026/11/27/used-cybertruck-prices-jump-after-tesla-kills-it/",
    "origin_publisher": "Electrek",
    "published_at": "2026-11-27T17:06:53+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-11-27T17:06:53+00:00",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-11-27T19:20:09.911258+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-11-27T19:20:09.911258+00:00",
    "body": "A used 2026 Cyberbeast listed at $150,790 (Screenshot: CarGurus)\nUsed Cybertruck prices are up 10 to 15% in the month since Tesla announced it would end production of its electric pickup, according to listing data from CarGurus and Visor.\nSome dealers are pushing it much further. One in New Jersey now wants $150,790 for a used Cyberbeast, about $36,000 more than Tesla charges for a brand-new one.\nThe numbers\nCarGurus’ price index puts the average used Cybertruck at $89,715, up 9.84% over the last 30 days and 13.5% over 90 days. Over the same 30 days, the broader used car market went down 1.37%.\nVisor, which tracks dealer listings hourly and compares the same trucks month to month, shows a bigger move: +15% like-for-like versus last month, with an average asking price of $101,784 across 171 used trucks. Listings that sold in the last 30 days took 17 days on average.\nAdvertisement - scroll for more content\nThe curve on CarGurus’ chart is flat at around $80,000 from July to late October. Then it bends up in November.\nThat lines up exactly with Tesla’s moves. On November 6, Musk said Tesla would wind down Cybertruck production at Giga Texas, and Tesla raised prices by up to 10% the same day . A week later, Tesla stopped selling every Cybertruck except the Cyberbeast at $109,990. And this week, it bumped the Cyberbeast again to $114,990, which is what Tesla’s configurator shows today.\nIn early August, the cheapest Cybertruck you could order was $69,990. Now it’s $114,990.\nThe craziest listing\nSorting CarGurus’ nationwide used Cybertruck inventory by price, the top listing is a 2026 Cyberbeast with 4,493 miles at J&S AutoHaus in Ewing, New Jersey. The asking price is $150,790 with fees.\nA used 2026 Cyberbeast listed at $150,790 (Screenshot: CarGurus)\nCarGurus flags it as “Overpriced” and says it’s $35,773 above market. But the price history is the best part.\nThe dealer listed it at $134,995 on November 20. It went to $139,995 on November 25, the day after Tesla’s latest $5,000 Cyberbeast hike, and then to $149,995 the next day. That’s $15,000 in six days on a truck nobody has saved yet, according to CarGurus.\nThe same dealer also has a 2026 Cyberbeast with 851 miles at $145,790. Elsewhere, a 2024 Cyberbeast with 302 miles in Calumet City, Illinois, is listed at $148,816. And a 2024 dual-motor AWD with 1,500 miles in Lincoln, Nebraska, is at $145,306. That’s more than the $99,990 Tesla charged for a fully loaded Foundation Series AWD back in 2024.\nCanadian dealers are playing the same game. The most expensive listing on AutoTrader.ca right now is a 2024 Cyberbeast with 1 km in Mississauga at C$199,999. The other 13 priced listings in the country sit between C$105,000 and C$141,995.\nThe other end of the market\nSort the same CarGurus inventory from the bottom, though, and the picture changes.\nA 2025 AWD with 22,495 miles is listed at $68,619 in San Bernardino, California. A 2024 Cyberbeast with 65,482 miles is at $70,118. There are several 2024 AWD trucks with 20,000 to 40,000 miles in the $77,000 to $80,000 range, and CarGurus rates many of them as good or great deals.\nSo the floor didn’t move all that much. What moved is the top and the middle.\nThere’s another detail in the CarGurus data. The 2024 model year is up 11.68% in 30 days, to $91,220. The 2025 model year is flat at $80,979, with 0% change over the same period.\nThe 2024 trucks include the Foundation Series, which came with Tesla’s “Full Self-Driving” package bought outright. Tesla now only sells FSD as a subscription, so those trucks have something new ones don’t. The 2025s, mostly the cheaper AWD trims, aren’t getting the same bump.\nElectrek’s Take\nIt’s good news for current owners who want out. Many of them watched their trucks lose about half their value between 2024 and 2025. This is the best window they’ve had in a long time.\nTop comment by Philip234\nLiked by 4 people\nUm no. ASKING prices are up. Transaction prices? They will resume their cratering momentarily.\nView all comments\nBut we wouldn’t read it as a sudden wave of love for the Cybertruck. Tesla made the cheaper versions impossible to order, so used trucks are now the only way to get anything other than a $115,000 Cyberbeast. That pushes the middle of the market up. And part of it is just dealers testing what people will pay. These are asking prices.\nThe bigger question is what happens after production ends. Parts and service for a low-volume stainless-steel truck that Tesla no longer makes. We’d expect that to weigh on used values far more than a configurator price hike helps them.\nIf you own one and you’ve been thinking about selling, now looks like a good time. If you’re buying, don’t pay discontinuation hype prices.\nIf you’re buying an EV, used or new, powering it with home solar can cut your fuel costs to nearly zero. With electricity rates up almost 10% last year and expected to keep climbing, going solar is one of the best ways to protect yourself against rising costs. And with lease and PPA options, you can do it with zero upfront cost and start saving immediately. If you want to find the best deal, check out EnergySage . It’s a free service with hundreds of pre-vetted installers competing for your business, so you save 20 to 30% compared to going it alone. No sales calls until you pick an installer. Get your free quotes here .\nFTC: We use income earning auto affiliate links. More.\nStay up to date with the latest content by subscribing to Electrek on Google News . You’re reading Electrek— experts who break news about Tesla , electric vehicles, and green energy , day after day. Be sure to check out our homepage for all the latest news, and follow Electrek on X , Facebook , and LinkedIn to stay in the loop. Don’t know where to start? Check out our YouTube channel for the latest reviews.",
